{"name":"Earth Crust","hex":"#8c4f42","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"earth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Earth (“the third planet of the Solar System, upon which humans live”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Soil.","Any general rock-based material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earth"},{"term":"crust","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A more solid, dense or hard layer on a surface or boundary.","The external, hardened layer of certain foodstuffs, including most types of bread, fried meat, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with a crust.","To form a crust."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crust"}],"entries":[{"title":"Earth's crust","blocks":[{"text":"Earth's crust is its outer shell of rock, comprising less than one percent of the planet's radius and volume. It is the top component of the lithosphere, a solidified division of Earth's layers that includes the crust and the upper part of the mantle. The lithosphere is broken into tectonic plates whose motion allows heat to escape the interior of Earth into space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Tectonic_plates_%282022%29.svg/330px-Tectonic_plates_%282022%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth's_crust","wikidata":"Q15316"},{"title":"Crust (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, the crust is the outermost solid shell of a planet, dwarf planet, or natural satellite. It is usually distinguished from the underlying mantle by its chemical makeup; however, in the case of icy satellites, it may be defined based on its phase."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Earth_Internal_Structure.svg/330px-Earth_Internal_Structure.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crust_(geology)","wikidata":"Q4232578"},{"title":"Earth","blocks":[{"text":"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only astronomical object known to harbor life. This is made possible by Earth being an ocean world, the only one in the Solar System sustaining liquid surface water. Almost all of Earth's water is contained in its ocean, which covers 70.8% of Earth's crust. The remaining 29.2% of Earth's crust is dry land, which is predominantly located within Earth's land hemisphere in the form of continental landmasses. Most of Earth's land is at least somewhat humid and covered by vegetation, while large ice sheets at Earth's polar deserts retain more water than Earth's groundwater, lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water combined. Earth's crust consists of slowly moving tectonic plates, which interact to produce mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes. Earth has a liquid outer core that generates a magnetosphere capable of deflecting most of the destructive solar winds and cosmic radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg/330px-Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth","wikidata":"Q2"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/World_geologic_provinces.jpg/500px-World_geologic_provinces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/World_geologic_provinces.jpg/500px-World_geologic_provinces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Geologic provinces of the world (USGS)\n@media(min-width:720px){.mw-parser-output .columns-start .column{float:left;min-width:20em}.mw-parser-output .columns-2 .column{width:50%}.mw-parser-output .columns-3 .column{width:33.3%}.mw-parser-output .columns-4 .column{width:25%}.mw-parser-output .columns-5 .column{width:20%}}\n.mw-parser-output .legend{page-break-inside:avoid;break-inside:avoid-column}.mw-parser-output .legend-color{display:inline-block;min-width:1.25em;height:1.25em;line-height:1.25;margin:1px 0;text-align:center;border:1px solid black;background-color:transparent;color:black}.mw-parser-output .legend-text{}  Shield\n  Platform\n  Orogen\n  Basin\n  Large igneous province\n  Extended crust\n\nOceanic crust:  0–20 Ma\n  20–65 Ma\n  >65 Ma\n","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWorld_geologic_provinces.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Differentiation_of_Earth.jpg/500px-MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Differentiation_of_Earth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Differentiation_of_Earth.jpg/500px-MIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Differentiation_of_Earth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Heavier elements like iron move towards the center of the earth while lighter materials rise to the outer layers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_12.007_Geobiology%2C_Spring_2007_-_Differentiation_of_Earth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/500px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/1280px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/500px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_th